 "cushionNotice": "Stack the rows. A Denver parent raising a child with intensive needs meets a job loss, a lapse in Medicaid or a closed child-care center with almost no cushion — and each of those can turn into a child-welfare case (Barrier 3). A Douglas County parent meets the same child's crisis with a salary, private insurance, paid leave, a second car, a relative with room. **The child's need can be the same. The cost is not.** And the suburbs are not one place: Arapahoe's child poverty (10.4%) is close to the state's, and its uninsured rate is about Denver's, while Douglas's are less than half of Arapahoe's. Some RJ families live on the thin side of that line.",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Private insurance, paid leave, a parent who can leave work for a meeting, a lawyer if one is needed — these cushions decide whether a family's crisis becomes a case file. Inside them, another family's case looks like a choice. See 2c."
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical — *I drive past this every day.*",
   "text": "I-25, C-470 and E-470 carry suburban families to downtown games, concerts and the airport without crossing a neighborhood. Tennyson sits on a trendy street of shops and restaurants that looks like home; nothing marks it. **The fence here is not distance. It is that a child's crisis is invisible from the street — in Berkeley and in Highlands Ranch.** Tennyson serves children from Douglas and Arapahoe too. ✅"
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "Bad kids. Behavior is a child's way of saying what happened. We also arrive thinking foster care means abuse; most findings are neglect, and nearly all involve poverty (Barrier 3). We assume the system has a place for every child; it doesn't (Barrier 1). **Babying or excusing children**, then learning from staff that holding a child to high standards is a form of respect."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Fear beforehand (some students expected the week to be awful). Then helplessness after one week — the wish to have made a difference. Many of these children have already lost seven or eight placements' worth of adults (Barrier 4); a week-long visitor who leaves is one more goodbye. The honest goal is the one a leader gave the group: show up, be steady, and let a child have a good week with adults who are kind. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what staff told you, and what you concluded. **Shyness and waiting to be approached** — the assumption that a child who wants to be seen will come to you; many of these children have learned not to. **Turning inward:** in RJ's Denver reflections, a question about the people at the site is most often answered about the student's own stress or loneliness. That is honest, and it can also crowd the children out. **Guilt about privilege** — feeling conspicuous in clean clothes — which is about us, not them. **Comparison to home** (our own childhood, our own parents at pickup), useful when it opens a question, a fence when it closes one."
  },
  {
   "h": "Convenience",
   "text": "For juniors, a Denver site can be chosen because it is close to home, or treated as hours to finish; students say so honestly. The fence is not laziness; it is that service near home can feel like a break from life rather than a look at it."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed — reading our family's stability as God's favor and a child's trauma as its absence. Everything happens for a reason — a sentence that, said to a child in care, explains away what adults did. Saving children — Tennyson does not need rescuers; it needs adults who stay."
  }
 ],
